\chapter{Scheduling, synchronization, and native IPC kernels}
\label{ch:104}
\index{Scheduling, synchronization, and native IPC kernels}

MuLIB supplies native fibers and channels reused by the private On1x C11 cooperative runtime. The scheduler owns dispatch and blocked waits, while On1x supplies checked lexical and affine policies at source level. Native framed IPC handles a separate connection and wire lifecycle. OS-backed native synchronization and source cooperative scheduling make different assumptions about blocking and thread ownership.

\section{Pick an ordering oracle}
For a rendezvous, have the sender and receiver observe progress before and after transfer. For a bounded queue, make a producer wait on capacity and then unblock it after a receive. Verify cancellation while a wait is registered, and check close after the last sender. Make each test use deterministic coordination instead of sleeps where ordering is being asserted. A C test can verify the primitive's scheduling behavior. A source test should also exercise the On1x handle and endpoint lifetime rules. \cref{ch:41} through \cref{ch:46} cover the checked source profile.

\section{Reconstruct the rendezvous without a stopwatch}
In the cooperative channels example the descriptor has one sender,
one receiver and capacity zero. After splitting it, the worker captures
the sender; the parent still has the receiver. The worker sends one,
two and three, printing each number on successful send. The parent
receives three times and prints ten times the received value. Write
two columns for the trace. In the worker column the successful values
are $1,2,3$; in the parent column the received products are $10,20,30$.
Each send needs a matching receive, since no unpaired value can occupy
a buffer slot. That constraint alone does not say which participant's
post-transfer print must run first. Do not derive a portable scheduling
rule from the order of two adjacent print statements in a single run.

After the third transfer the worker closes its sender and returns seven.
The parent performs a fourth receive. No sender remains to deliver a
fourth value, so this receive takes the None branch and prints zero.
Joining the worker prints seven; it consumes the task handle, not a
channel element. Those last two observations distinguish closing the
channel from completing the task. An implementation that delivered the
return value as a message would break both checks.

For a debugging exercise move the join ahead of the first receive.
The parent would wait for a worker whose first send needs the parent
to receive. No amount of increasing the native thread's time slice
supplies the missing communication partner. Restore the receive before
waiting for task completion.

